speechtext:
On that point, Mr. Chairman, if I may trespass upon the time of the committee for a moment, I think the minister recognizes that our concern is that it be done under the ordinary procedures of the house. The minister did not quite give that guarantee. There is close to the surface all this suspicion that has been generated over 60 years. We have to establish a statutory provision.
[Mr. Hamilton.}
Then, this matter would be written into the statute. It would not seriously affect the house rules, but it would impress upon the people who think we are going blind into this new era and turning powers over to a super-board, that we are guaranteeing by statute that this body will not get out of complete touch with parliament. That is the whole point of the amendment; we want a guarantee in this respect.
